00:00:26.372 --> 00:00:27.830
PROFESSOR: So let
me just start out

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:31.880
by giving you a quick overview
of what we're going to be doing

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.460
today, what the plan is.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.910
I'm going to spend the first
maybe third or so of the time

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.330
or maybe hopefully less than
that talking very generally

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.073
about basic camera
concepts, and if you

00:00:45.073 --> 00:00:47.240
have some video experience
or if you have some still

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.490
photography experience,
they're going

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:50.930
to be pretty familiar to you.

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:52.430
I'm going to keep
it fairly general,

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.263
and we're going to go
through fairly quickly

00:00:54.263 --> 00:00:59.920
some basic things in order to
lay the groundwork for the more

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.920
exciting and interesting
part, which is actually

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.477
going out and shooting.

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:06.810
But after I finish that, then
Dave is going to take over

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.870
and he's going to provide you
with a very quick introduction

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.930
to our camera, where
the controls are,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.560
how things are set up, how
to do some of the things

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.410
that I'm talking about
specifically with our camera.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.500
And then we're all going
to go out and have a chance

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.200
to actually shoot some stuff,
which should be a lot of fun.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.230
So that's the story, and I'm
just going to jump right in.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:33.000
Although I guess I
should ask first,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.620
does anyone have any questions
or thoughts from Wednesday

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:36.120
night?

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:41.300
OK, well, what
we're trying to do

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.050
is build every session
on the last one.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:51.050
And so I'm going to start
out by referencing something

00:01:51.050 --> 00:01:52.910
that I talked about last time.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:56.840
The way that we began, which was
looking at another visual art.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.720
Painting.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:03.770
And if I asked you what
the tools and the medium

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:08.370
of a painter were, you would
have a pretty easy answer,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:08.870
right?

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:11.870
I mean, it's paint, it's canvas.

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:14.180
It's the stuff that you
use to make a painting.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.760
Same with sculpture.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.540
Stone, steel, mud, bricks,
whatever it may be.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:26.980
So the question then is,
what's the medium for film

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.630
and what are the tools of film?

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.730
Some are obvious, obviously.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:35.080
The camera, exactly.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:37.330
That's kind of the brush.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:41.410
I mean, not exactly, but
that's the basic tool.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:42.190
But what else?

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.410
What are the other
tools of the filmmaker?

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:46.790
Light.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:47.290
Others?

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.880
So the actual what
you record on.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.160
Exactly, and how
you manipulate that.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:02.020
But there are lots
of others as well,

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.390
and a concept that I find really
useful and interesting in just

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.478
thinking about what you do as
a filmmaker or a video maker,

00:03:09.478 --> 00:03:11.520
and I'm going to use those
terms interchangeably,

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:16.320
as I said on Wednesday, are
what we call or it can be called

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.270
the plastic elements of film.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:21.030
And by that, I just
mean the elements

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:25.290
that you as the artist, the
filmmaker have control over

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:28.870
and that you can change at will.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:29.940
So the camera.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:33.330
What you choose to do with the
camera is, obviously, extremely

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:37.380
important and in fact
central to the outcome

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:41.610
and to what you want to do as
an artist or as a documentarian.

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.900
Lighting, again.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.870
Whether you're actually adding
lights, setting up lights,

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.180
doing very complex
lighting setups

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.890
or just using available light.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.990
How you choose to place
your subject in relation

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:56.970
to the sun, how you choose to
light the lights in a room.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.440
These fluorescent
lights above us.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.340
We're taping this
for OpenCourseWare,

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.530
and before everyone
came, we tried

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.800
a couple of different settings
for the overhead lights.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.550
And so that is, again,
a plastic element.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.610
But there are others too.

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.530
Can you think of others?

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.320
The lens, yeah.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.355
What lens you use to
shoot with, exactly.

00:04:20.355 --> 00:04:20.855
Others?

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:27.080
Yeah, exactly.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.360
The scene, the setting.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.500
Could be the location.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:31.950
Another way of
thinking about it.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.850
So if you're doing-- and that's
obviously true of a fiction

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.620
film, but it's also
true of a documentary.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.808
If you're interviewing somebody,
where do you choose to put them

00:04:40.808 --> 00:04:42.350
and what does where
you choose to put

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.240
them say about your subject?

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.380
What do you want it to say?

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.638
You have control over
that and you have--

00:04:48.638 --> 00:04:50.930
in a lot of ways, you have
as much or as little control

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:54.950
as you want, because you
can be very interventionist

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:58.760
and place them exactly where
you want them or you can just

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.340
do whatever happens.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.920
If you went to interview
a professor here at MIT

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.750
and you found them
in their office,

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.290
you just could do
it right there.

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:10.370
And that would say something
about them as well.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.430
Do you clean the office or
do you not clean the office?

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.500
That's another thing.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:17.300
So if you think
about it like that,

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:20.480
then suddenly, this whole world
of elements opens up, right?

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.040
Props, how you arrange
things in a scene.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.660
Whether you dress
the set, and again,

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.090
whether it's a documentary or a
fiction film, it still applies.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:33.480
Sound.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.210
How you record the sound,
whether you record the sound,

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:39.720
what you do in editing in
order to change things,

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.210
if you add music, if you
mix the sound, if you add

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:47.250
sound effects, and editing
itself is a plastic element.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.680
That's almost as
important as the camera.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.680
It's a central tool
for the filmmaker,

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.370
how you edit a piece.

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.460
So there are any number--

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.290
and these are just a few.

00:06:00.290 --> 00:06:03.240
Any number of plastic
elements that you can control,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.170
and I think this is just
like a central idea that

00:06:06.170 --> 00:06:09.500
seems obvious, but
it's also something

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.990
that you want to
continually remind yourself,

00:06:11.990 --> 00:06:18.470
because I know that in my
experience as a producer

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:23.750
or a director and doing a lot
of other things in film as well,

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:27.680
you can sometimes forget
that you have the ability

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.960
to change things, because
sometimes you're rushed,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.580
you don't have time, or
fate deals you a blow

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:38.030
that you feel you
have to accept.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.720
But you just remember that
you can take the initiative

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:42.860
and change something.

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:46.220
So if the location doesn't
work, the beautiful idea you had

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:48.980
is not going to happen.

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:50.690
Improvise and think
of something else.

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:54.060
You have the power to change
a lot of these things.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.440
So today, we're going
to talk about camera,

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:01.370
which is in many ways, first and
foremost, the plastic element.

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:04.190
It's the first one that was
mentioned here when I asked you

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:07.130
about what kinds of
things you use as a tool,

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:10.880
and it's obviously
the fundamental thing,

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:13.980
because everything is
passing through the lens.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:16.820
And I want to just provide a
very, very brief introduction

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:21.140
to some of the really basic
ideas in filmmaking, which

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:24.410
are very similar in many ways
to some of the basic ideas

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.080
in still photography
if you've done that.

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:30.410
And one way to think about them,
one way I like to think about

00:07:30.410 --> 00:07:33.080
them are as the four F's.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:38.450
F-stop, or exposure, focus,
focal length, and framing.

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:42.050
And there are others, but
they don't start with F,

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:44.690
but I like to start with these.

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:47.612
And F-stop is also
maybe a bit simplistic

00:07:47.612 --> 00:07:49.070
when you're talking
about exposure.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:50.612
But it's just a way
to remember them.

00:07:50.612 --> 00:07:53.030
F-stop, focus, focal
length, framing.

00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:56.600
And I'm just going to spend a
couple of minutes on each one,

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.192
and don't expect to
absorb it all right now.

00:07:59.192 --> 00:08:00.650
It's the kind of
thing that's going

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:02.525
to make a lot more sense
when you're filming.

00:08:02.525 --> 00:08:05.480
So I'm not going to get really
hung up on a lot of details.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:08.120
This isn't a class on
optics or the physics

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:09.920
of light or anything
like that, but I

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:12.780
want to just introduce
a couple of terms.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:14.570
So first, exposure.

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:18.740
F-stop or aperture is one
of the basic things that

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:22.730
controls what your
exposure of your camera is,

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:26.330
and that is basically
in simplistic terms

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:29.600
or in simple terms how much
light is entering the camera

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:30.140
lens.

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:32.720
How much light is
hitting the sensor

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:34.850
that's recording the image?

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:39.179
And lenses are equipped with
an aperture that is adjustable.

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:41.750
So you see at the
top there, there's

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:44.210
an aperture that's very
wide, it's very open,

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:49.850
which in terms of F-stop
is a very low number, F2,

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:52.010
and at the bottom of
that very same lens,

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.470
the aperture is almost
completely shut.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:57.830
It's just almost a pinhole,
and that corresponds

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:01.460
to a very high F, number F22.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:05.000
And lenses, you'll see that
the lenses on our cameras

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.590
can be adjusted.

00:09:06.590 --> 00:09:09.050
You can either leave
it in auto and let

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.960
the camera choose what
the exposure should be,

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:13.220
or you can choose it.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.800
And obviously,
the more light you

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:19.910
have in a scene, the more
stop down or the smaller

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:23.040
the aperture would be in order
to get the correct exposure.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.700
In other words, in order to
have enough light hitting

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.340
your sensor but
not too much light

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:32.180
in order to get a
good looking image.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:37.400
Just also quickly, F-stop
is not the only way

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:38.630
to adjust exposure.

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:40.100
You do have other controls.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:41.760
You have the shutter speed.

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:48.380
The shutter speed is basically
determining how much time

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.630
each frame of your
video or your film

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:54.290
is exposed to light
as it goes by.

00:09:54.290 --> 00:09:59.540
And you can adjust it, but
in this video and film,

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:01.400
you normally aren't
going to adjust it.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:04.790
You normally want to keep it
for normal appearing motion

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:07.500
at about 1/60 of a second.

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:09.620
So in practical
terms, unless you're

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:12.200
shooting something
that's a very high speed

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:15.380
activity or some kind
of special situation

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.740
or you're maybe in a very,
very low light situation,

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:21.860
you don't want to use the
shutter as an exposure control.

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.780
Mainly, you're going
to keep it at 160.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:24.290
Yes, sir?

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.670
TA: It's usually
double the frame rate.

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:29.550
So if it's like if you're
shooting 24 frames per second,

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.863
it would be 1 over 48.

00:10:31.863 --> 00:10:34.280
PROFESSOR: Right, and we're
shooting 30 frames per second,

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.610
so that's why it's 160, yeah.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.340
The second thing is the
camera sensitivity itself.

00:10:40.340 --> 00:10:43.040
Different cameras have a
different sensitivity to light,

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:48.080
and you'll very quickly come to
both appreciate and hate some

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:49.880
of the quirks of our camera.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:53.150
It is sensitive compared
to some and not as others,

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:55.910
so it depends on what
you may be expecting.

00:10:55.910 --> 00:11:00.230
You can also, however, there
are adjustments to cameras

00:11:00.230 --> 00:11:01.670
and video cameras basically.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:04.790
It's called the gain control.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:07.490
So if the lighting
situation is very poor,

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:11.040
you don't have a lot of
light, the gain can be raised.

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:15.440
But the trade off is that the
image gets very, very fuzzy.

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:17.400
Much lower resolution.

00:11:17.400 --> 00:11:18.920
But there is that sensitivity.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:22.190
And then finally, of
course, there are lights.

00:11:22.190 --> 00:11:23.960
You can add light.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:27.403
Either by changing the
location, going from--

00:11:27.403 --> 00:11:29.570
if one room is too dark and
the next room isn't, you

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:30.650
can change the rooms.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.180
If you have lights,
you can add them.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:35.270
We actually are--
for this class,

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.460
we're shooting available
light for the most part.

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:39.200
There will be one
class on lighting,

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.030
but we don't have
lighting packages that

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:42.530
are going to be
generally available,

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:47.900
but there are ways
to overcome that clip

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:52.850
lights, regular consumer
lights, and people in this class

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:55.880
have done interesting things
with those kinds of things

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:57.020
in the past.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:59.510
So anyway, lots of ways
to control exposure,

00:11:59.510 --> 00:12:04.040
the essential basic one
is the F stopper aperture.

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:08.480
So let's go on to focus, the
second F. First thing to know

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:12.080
is that just like with
exposure, this camera and almost

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:15.602
all video cameras have two
settings, auto and manual.

00:12:15.602 --> 00:12:20.570
Manual focus is almost always
preferred, because just as

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:25.520
with exposure, the auto
setting can be fooled.

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:28.485
It will breathe, so
it'll change its mind

00:12:28.485 --> 00:12:30.860
and focus on something else
in the middle of a shot which

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:33.200
can be very distracting
and annoying.

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:35.640
So in almost all
situations you're

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:39.860
going to want to stay manual,
both for F-stop and for focus.

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:41.150
But you do have that option.

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:43.400
You do have auto as
a choice, and there

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:46.460
are situations, where
it's better to be on auto

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:47.780
than to be nothing, right?

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:52.880
I mean, if you grab the camera
because there's a flying saucer

00:12:52.880 --> 00:12:56.300
landing outside and you want to
get the shot, if it's an auto,

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.540
it's much better to have
something than to have nothing

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:02.450
and to be adjusting.

00:13:02.450 --> 00:13:07.040
But generally, we discourage
the use of auto focus and auto

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:09.170
iris.

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:12.980
Second thing to think about with
focus is the depth of field.

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:15.500
Depth of field is
basically a term

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:19.790
that refers to how much of your
image in terms of the depth

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:21.120
is in focus?

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:23.870
So if you're shooting
me sitting here

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:28.920
talking as our camera person
is, hopefully, I'm in focus.

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:32.180
The background behind me
doesn't have to be in focus,

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:34.010
and it might or
might not be in focus

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:37.550
depending on the aperture
that the camera is set up.

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:42.140
So a very wide aperture because
it's a low light situation

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:44.300
means that you have
very shallow focus,

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.940
and the background
may be out of focus.

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:50.660
The smaller the aperture
gets, the deeper your focus

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:54.440
is so that the background
suddenly could become in focus.

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.080
And this is something that you
can-- it's an aesthetic choice.

00:13:57.080 --> 00:13:59.852
It's not just determined
by your lighting,

00:13:59.852 --> 00:14:01.310
although that's
obviously something

00:14:01.310 --> 00:14:03.890
you have to deal with, but
you can actually make choices

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:06.860
about what's in focus and what's
not, and how deep the focus is

00:14:06.860 --> 00:14:11.240
and in it and it has an
impact on how people read

00:14:11.240 --> 00:14:13.970
the images that you create.

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:15.770
I don't if anybody
here is nearsighted,

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:18.200
but if you want to get a
sense of how depth of field

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:21.560
is determined by the aperture,
you can take your glasses off,

00:14:21.560 --> 00:14:24.980
and you can actually do this.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:27.080
And the smaller you
make the pinhole

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:31.640
that you're looking through,
the more in focus you are

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:32.840
or what you're seeing is.

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:35.090
And it doesn't really work
if you're everything's

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:37.140
always in focus for you.

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:38.880
But for me, it
works really well.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.920
So if I don't have my glasses in
the morning, I can look at you

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:45.770
and I can see that
you're quite sharp if I

00:14:45.770 --> 00:14:47.420
make the pinhole small enough.

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:49.040
So anyway, it's kind
of a cool effect.

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:51.740
And it gives you a sense that,
basically, what's happening

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:55.430
is that the smaller the aperture
that the light is passing

00:14:55.430 --> 00:14:59.570
through, the more aligned,
and again, this is not

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:02.480
an optics class and I'm not
going to go too far down

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:04.370
this road that I
don't know much about,

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:08.990
but the light is more
aligned, there's less scatter,

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:12.420
and that makes everything
in sharper focus.

00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:16.980
So again, focus is something
that is extremely important.

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:18.800
So is exposure.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:21.170
They're non-negotiable.

00:15:21.170 --> 00:15:24.350
The first two F's are, either
you did it or you didn't.

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:26.100
You're either in
focus or you're not.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.190
And you pretty much
want to be in focus

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:31.340
and you pretty much want
to be properly exposed.

00:15:31.340 --> 00:15:34.310
You don't want to have too
much light hitting your image,

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:36.470
you don't want to
have too little.

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:40.250
Even if you want to manipulate
focus or exposure later

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:42.920
because you want to actually
have things appear out

00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:44.630
of focus, it's much
better to do it

00:15:44.630 --> 00:15:47.180
in editing and
post-production than to do it

00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:50.390
when you're shooting, because
you can't change it back.

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:52.820
You can't make a
blurry image sharp.

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.860
You can make a
sharp image blurry.

00:15:54.860 --> 00:15:57.110
You can take a very
well exposed image

00:15:57.110 --> 00:16:00.620
and make it dark or bright,
but you can't very easily

00:16:00.620 --> 00:16:03.320
take a dark image and
make it properly exposed.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:05.870
So these first two,
as I said, I usually

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:09.950
refer to them as the
non-negotiable two.

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:15.830
The next two, however,
are infinitely negotiable.

00:16:15.830 --> 00:16:20.780
Focal length refers
to whether you're

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:23.060
shooting something
in a wide angle

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:25.980
or a telephoto or
a normal setting.

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.910
And again, you may
have some experience

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:31.280
with still photography.

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:34.040
Wide angle lens
are the lenses that

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:35.900
are a smaller number
of millimeters,

00:16:35.900 --> 00:16:41.660
like the 12 millimeter lens,
the 6 millimeter lens, the 16

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:42.740
millimeter lens.

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:45.140
Telephoto are the big numbers.

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:48.890
200, 300, and 150.

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:54.470
And each of them give you very,
very, very different feeling

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:56.130
kinds of images.

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.130
The wide angle lens gives
you a much wider angle

00:16:58.130 --> 00:16:59.760
of view, obviously.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:03.141
So if you're filming
landscapes, you

00:17:03.141 --> 00:17:04.849
can put a wide angle
lens on your camera,

00:17:04.849 --> 00:17:08.790
and from the same position,
get a much wider field.

00:17:08.790 --> 00:17:10.760
It also gives you
much deeper focus.

00:17:10.760 --> 00:17:14.839
To go back to the idea of depth
of field, with a wide angle

00:17:14.839 --> 00:17:17.960
lens, it's much easier to
get everything in focus

00:17:17.960 --> 00:17:20.420
from the foreground
to the background.

00:17:20.420 --> 00:17:22.950
Telephoto lenses,
on the other hand,

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:24.480
are a much narrower
angle of view.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.230
So if I'm standing in the same
place filming the same thing,

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:31.770
I'm seeing a much
narrower field,

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.880
which can also be thought
of as magnification, right?

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:38.720
So same image, but now, if I'm
filming the camera person who's

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:41.840
filming me, I'm
seeing just his face

00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:44.270
instead of the whole field.

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:46.640
And the focus, the depth of
field, is much shallower.

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:48.980
So it's much easier to
get a nice soft background

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:51.410
on a telephoto lens.

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:53.150
Just one other note
about focal length.

00:17:53.150 --> 00:17:57.260
Again, it's something that
is infinitely variable

00:17:57.260 --> 00:17:58.910
and you can choose
as a filmmaker

00:17:58.910 --> 00:18:02.930
what kinds of focal lengths
you use, but don't confuse

00:18:02.930 --> 00:18:06.500
a wide angle lens with a
wide shot or a telephoto lens

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:07.730
with a close up.

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:09.980
It's easier to get a close
up with a telephoto lens

00:18:09.980 --> 00:18:12.560
and it's easier to get a wide
shot with a wide angle lens,

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:16.770
but it's certainly possible to
do a wide shot with a telephoto

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:17.270
lens.

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:19.937
You just have to be much farther
away from your subject and vise

00:18:19.937 --> 00:18:20.630
versa.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:23.180
It's very possible to do a
close up with a wide angle lens,

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:24.850
you just have to
get much closer.

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:26.960
But they'll feel
very, very different,

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:31.680
and I'll show you some extreme
examples here in a minute.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:34.490
So this is an extreme
wide angle lens.

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:36.590
What we call a fish eye lens.

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:42.230
And you'll see, first of all,
that it's very distorted,

00:18:42.230 --> 00:18:44.527
but you're seeing a very,
very wide field of view.

00:18:44.527 --> 00:18:46.610
So wide, in fact, that the
only way to accommodate

00:18:46.610 --> 00:18:48.590
is the distortion.

00:18:48.590 --> 00:18:51.290
You're also seeing
incredible depth of field.

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:53.210
The background, the
infinite distance

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:56.180
is sharp, and so is
the hand that's not

00:18:56.180 --> 00:18:59.330
that far away from the camera.

00:18:59.330 --> 00:19:03.740
It also wide angle lenses--
and this is an extreme example.

00:19:03.740 --> 00:19:07.190
Wide angle lenses tend
to distort and elongate

00:19:07.190 --> 00:19:08.940
the distance between objects.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:13.010
So that hand is much closer to
the camera than it might appear

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:16.910
and the distance between the
hand and every other element

00:19:16.910 --> 00:19:19.370
going back is
somewhat elongated.

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:23.090
So it makes smaller
rooms feel much bigger,

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:24.845
it makes distances
seem much greater.

00:19:27.590 --> 00:19:29.100
Telephoto lens.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:32.010
The long lens, because
they're long, do the opposite.

00:19:32.010 --> 00:19:36.410
So here, we have a
telephoto shot of a bird,

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:39.410
and you see first of all that
the background is very, very

00:19:39.410 --> 00:19:43.940
soft, even though it's not that
far really behind the bird,

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:45.530
and things are very compressed.

00:19:45.530 --> 00:19:50.180
So the distance between
the bird and the background

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:53.280
feels like there's not
much distance at all,

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:56.870
even though there might
actually be quite a lot.

00:19:56.870 --> 00:19:58.670
One of the things
about telephoto lenses

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:00.950
is that sometimes
the depth of field

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:03.320
is so shallow that
you can literally

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:10.870
have the eye of the bird in
focus and the background that

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:14.120
of the plant that it's standing
on completely soft here.

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:16.340
The plant is actually in focus.

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:17.990
But if you're
doing an interview,

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:20.990
a human eye might be
in focus and their ear

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:25.880
might be out of focus at
an extreme telephoto lens.

00:20:25.880 --> 00:20:29.030
So this is actually
aesthetically really pleasing,

00:20:29.030 --> 00:20:32.280
because the foreground
jumps out at you.

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:34.010
Our eye goes
immediately to the bird

00:20:34.010 --> 00:20:35.840
and the background
just drops off

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:40.820
into this very soft,
nice backplate.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:44.840
So again, I could shoot
a wide shot like this,

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:46.790
but it would look
extremely different

00:20:46.790 --> 00:20:49.670
than shooting a wide shot
with a wide angle lens.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.410
So now, let's talk
about framing.

00:20:55.410 --> 00:20:59.370
And framing, this is really--

00:20:59.370 --> 00:21:02.190
once you master the basics
of the camera framing

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:06.720
is a catchall term for
everything else about how

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:09.240
you choose to shoot things.

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:10.710
Do you choose to
shoot close ups?

00:21:10.710 --> 00:21:12.150
Do you shoot choose
to shoot wide shots?

00:21:12.150 --> 00:21:13.320
What's the grammar of film?

00:21:13.320 --> 00:21:18.150
And we're not going to do
any kind of exhaustive talk

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:22.950
about that right now,
but it's an infinitely

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:27.840
interesting and
inexhaustible subject.

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:30.772
Sabrina, you mentioned
that on Wednesday night,

00:21:30.772 --> 00:21:32.730
when we were looking at
those very early films,

00:21:32.730 --> 00:21:34.393
everything was
filmed head to toe.

00:21:34.393 --> 00:21:36.060
And that was because
I mean there really

00:21:36.060 --> 00:21:38.940
hadn't been a grammar of
film developed at that point.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:42.120
They were just thinking
about them as stage plays.

00:21:42.120 --> 00:21:44.910
So people would walk in head to
toe and they'd do their thing

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:46.500
and they'd walk out.

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:48.450
But a few years
after that, people

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:53.310
started filming close ups, and
that was a huge revolution.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:56.400
They started filming shots
of the hands of people

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:57.390
doing things.

00:21:57.390 --> 00:21:59.040
They started moving
the camera so

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:02.310
that it would pan from one
person to another and back.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:04.360
They started filming
shots with someone

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.360
in the foreground and
someone in the background,

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.340
and then they'd do a
reverse with this person

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:11.100
in the foreground and this
person in the background.

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:14.938
And that kind of opened up this
whole language that all of us--

00:22:14.938 --> 00:22:16.980
we're used to reading this
language because we've

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:19.320
been doing it our entire
lives, but at that point,

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:20.520
it was an extremely new.

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:22.740
What shot works with this shot?

00:22:22.740 --> 00:22:24.820
What's confusing,
what's not confusing?

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:26.610
How do we understand a scene?

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:29.550
And all of that is
related to framing.

00:22:29.550 --> 00:22:33.210
So we're going to be looking at
a lot of films in this class.

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:36.180
We're going to be talking about
why they were shot the way they

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:40.680
were shot, both in terms
of the social terms

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:44.350
and the technical terms,
the technological terms,

00:22:44.350 --> 00:22:47.550
but also in terms of the
framing and the grammar of them.

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:50.820
And it's something
that you're going

00:22:50.820 --> 00:22:53.100
to gain a lot of experience
and understanding

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:54.525
of as you make your own films.

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:07.480
But just for starters, I'm going
to talk a little bit about just

00:23:07.480 --> 00:23:10.030
some basic ideas and concepts
that you might want to think

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:14.410
about as you frame a scene.

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:18.020
Let's take this
class, for instance.

00:23:18.020 --> 00:23:20.710
How would I film this class?

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:23.290
There are an infinite number
of ways that I could do it.

00:23:23.290 --> 00:23:26.050
It's being filmed right now
for a very particular reason.

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:28.600
A very particular purpose,
OpenCourseWare is filming it,

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:30.640
and the way they're
filming it is

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:34.690
by focusing on me because
I'm the one talking

00:23:34.690 --> 00:23:38.530
and on the slides,
because it's information

00:23:38.530 --> 00:23:40.900
that they're trying to capture.

00:23:40.900 --> 00:23:43.250
If we were filming it
for other purposes,

00:23:43.250 --> 00:23:45.710
we could have a completely
different approach.

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:51.040
We could film it as a
documentary cinema verite

00:23:51.040 --> 00:23:55.870
direct cinema style, which is if
you remember the scene the John

00:23:55.870 --> 00:23:59.290
F. Kennedy documentary about
the primary in Wisconsin

00:23:59.290 --> 00:24:02.470
that we watched the clip
of on Wednesday night.

00:24:02.470 --> 00:24:06.250
That was all about the
experience of being there,

00:24:06.250 --> 00:24:08.170
and it had a very
particular point

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:12.070
of view, the experience
of Jackie Kennedy and JFK

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.950
in that room shaking
a million hands being

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:16.550
pushed by the crowd.

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:20.770
So if we were going to film
this class in that way,

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:23.230
we could use a handheld
camera, that camera

00:24:23.230 --> 00:24:26.110
could be completely mobile.

00:24:26.110 --> 00:24:29.980
If I'm not what's
important to the filmmaker,

00:24:29.980 --> 00:24:32.020
I might barely be in it.

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:36.790
Maybe it's a film about you
and they're following you

00:24:36.790 --> 00:24:39.280
in your day and I'm
just this droning

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:40.390
voice in the background.

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:42.460
What they're interested
in is your response

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:45.340
to the environment and
how to capture that.

00:24:45.340 --> 00:24:49.300
So it'd be completely
different way

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:52.145
of filming this and the
framing and the shot choices

00:24:52.145 --> 00:24:53.770
would be completely
different than what

00:24:53.770 --> 00:24:56.210
we're doing right now.

00:24:56.210 --> 00:24:58.540
We could film it with
multiple cameras.

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:00.670
We could film as we
did the other night.

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:03.880
We could have a camera
pointing at students,

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:07.150
camera in the background,
we can edit it together.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:10.960
How we shoot people,
whether we're shooting them

00:25:10.960 --> 00:25:15.340
in close ups or wide shots all
has a really significant impact

00:25:15.340 --> 00:25:17.120
on the story that we're telling.

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:19.540
And one of the things
with documentary film

00:25:19.540 --> 00:25:22.690
is that as a camera
person, again,

00:25:22.690 --> 00:25:26.950
let's say that we're doing a
day in the life of Sabrina.

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:32.740
As the story unfolds, we're
constantly making adjustments.

00:25:32.740 --> 00:25:35.780
We're constantly making new
choices about what to film.

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:39.490
So if we're filming
your experience

00:25:39.490 --> 00:25:42.130
of being in this class, as that
experience changes as a camera

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:44.980
person, the camera
person would need

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:49.090
to be sensitive to that change.

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:53.620
So let's say you got into a
discussion and a disagreement

00:25:53.620 --> 00:25:55.540
with Dave, our TA.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:57.790
Because you didn't agree
with his interpretation

00:25:57.790 --> 00:26:00.640
of something, which will happen.

00:26:00.640 --> 00:26:03.040
No, no.

00:26:03.040 --> 00:26:05.890
So suddenly, we would
be focusing on Dave

00:26:05.890 --> 00:26:07.880
and your response to Dave.

00:26:07.880 --> 00:26:12.010
So we would be in close ups on
you listening to Dave, looking

00:26:12.010 --> 00:26:14.110
unhappy, arguing back.

00:26:14.110 --> 00:26:17.390
Suddenly, we're on Dave, Dave
is talking back and forth,

00:26:17.390 --> 00:26:19.272
back and forth, and
me, the instructor

00:26:19.272 --> 00:26:21.730
who is being filmed right now,
I'm not even in the picture.

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:26.200
It doesn't matter what I'm
saying or thinking or doing.

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.240
Then we're the
camera person starts

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:30.730
looking for up reaction
shots of other students.

00:26:30.730 --> 00:26:33.970
Does everyone agree with or
does everyone agree with Dave

00:26:33.970 --> 00:26:35.320
and think you're wrong?

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.650
And as you make that
choice as a camera person,

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:40.960
it's going to impact
who you choose to film.

00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:48.820
So we touched on the idea
of fairness and accuracy

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:50.860
in the documentary film.

00:26:50.860 --> 00:26:53.650
What you choose to frame
and how you choose to frame

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:56.740
it has a huge, huge impact
on the story that you tell.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:27:00.820
So you can walk into that
situation I just described

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:04.248
and film it with
an agenda, like we

00:27:04.248 --> 00:27:06.040
want to make Sabrina
look like the smartest

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:07.400
person in the room.

00:27:07.400 --> 00:27:10.300
So then you film it
in a certain way,

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:16.330
or we want to show how
completely isolated she is,

00:27:16.330 --> 00:27:18.460
film it in a different way.

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:22.880
Or you could be very sensitive
and open to the situation

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:28.030
and adjust and try to be make an
accurate or fair representation

00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:29.710
of what's actually happening.

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:32.950
And believe me that both are
common in documentary film.

00:27:32.950 --> 00:27:36.250
I mean, going in with an
agenda is extremely common,

00:27:36.250 --> 00:27:40.630
and that's not the same as lying
quote, unquote "manipulating",

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:44.020
although there is an element
of manipulation to it.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:47.320
But generally, we do go in
with preconceived notions

00:27:47.320 --> 00:27:52.220
and it impacts how we frame
things and how we shoot things.

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:54.880
So anyway, that's
the overall thing.

00:27:54.880 --> 00:27:57.130
We're not going to talk
about a lot of film grammar

00:27:57.130 --> 00:27:59.470
right now because I could go
on for the rest of the time

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:02.830
and I'd rather get on to
the actual camera work.

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:03.790
But I am going to--

00:28:03.790 --> 00:28:07.750
I am going to just raise a
couple of things to think about

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:10.240
since you very soon have
cameras in your hands

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:14.470
and just basic kinds of
things to think about

00:28:14.470 --> 00:28:20.200
as you choose the shots
that you're going to film.

00:28:20.200 --> 00:28:22.660
First of all, there's
a real tendency when

00:28:22.660 --> 00:28:25.680
you get a camera for the
first time-- and I mean,

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:28.450
I'm sure everyone at this
point is filmed something

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:30.610
with a video camera.

00:28:30.610 --> 00:28:33.790
There's a real tendency to
want to capture everything.

00:28:33.790 --> 00:28:38.333
So we're in this room, we're
filming, and you think,

00:28:38.333 --> 00:28:40.000
oh, I'm going to get
everything, and you

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:43.420
start doing this, which is
basically moving continually,

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:45.460
because that's how
our eyes work, right?

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:48.080
I'm just looking around,
I see everything.

00:28:48.080 --> 00:28:50.530
So you just assume the
camera works that way, too.

00:28:50.530 --> 00:28:52.540
But what you end up doing
is capturing nothing,

00:28:52.540 --> 00:28:58.900
because with the camera you have
to fake the way your eye works,

00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:01.990
and the way your eye
works is I'm doing this

00:29:01.990 --> 00:29:04.990
and I'm seeing everything,
but I'm also continuously--

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:08.620
my brain is continuously
picking out things

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:11.500
and making close
ups out of the stuff

00:29:11.500 --> 00:29:13.310
that I see making
different angles.

00:29:13.310 --> 00:29:15.243
So when I was talking
about suddenly

00:29:15.243 --> 00:29:17.410
there was a close up of
even though I saw everything

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:18.783
else too.

00:29:18.783 --> 00:29:20.200
When I talked about
Dave, suddenly

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:22.630
there was a close up of
Dave in my head, right?

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:24.730
So the camera has
to do that, and you

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:26.530
have to make it do
that, which means

00:29:26.530 --> 00:29:30.160
that what they call hosing
down the room, which

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:34.270
is this continual I don't
want to miss anything

00:29:34.270 --> 00:29:36.850
so I'm just going
to keep doing this,

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:38.620
it just ends up with mush.

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:41.230
And you're not telling a
story and you're not really

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:43.150
getting any shots that
you're able to edit.

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:46.448
So you want to be deliberate
about what you're shooting

00:29:46.448 --> 00:29:48.490
and you want to think
about what you're shooting.

00:29:48.490 --> 00:29:52.840
What story am I
telling and what images

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:54.550
do I need to tell that story?

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:56.140
Do I need to focus on you?

00:29:56.140 --> 00:29:58.000
Do I need to focus on Dave?

00:29:58.000 --> 00:30:00.742
Do I need to shoot a wide
shot that establishes

00:30:00.742 --> 00:30:02.200
the geography of
the room so that I

00:30:02.200 --> 00:30:05.410
know where people are sitting.

00:30:05.410 --> 00:30:08.980
Second thing is
very basic thing.

00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:11.770
Hold your shots.

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:13.840
There's also a tendency
that goes along

00:30:13.840 --> 00:30:17.080
with this hosing down the
room to never really hold

00:30:17.080 --> 00:30:18.100
on anything.

00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:22.780
So I'm filming Sabrina,
but then suddenly, I

00:30:22.780 --> 00:30:24.910
want to go over here and
then I want to go back,

00:30:24.910 --> 00:30:28.480
and then I end up
with nothing again.

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:33.100
So you we want to make a choice
and then stick with it for

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:35.060
long enough that you
actually get something.

00:30:35.060 --> 00:30:37.030
So if I'm shooting
a close up of Chris

00:30:37.030 --> 00:30:39.850
listening to me
skeptically, I want

00:30:39.850 --> 00:30:43.990
to hold on for a certain
number of seconds

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:46.415
and then go back to the next
thing that I want to shoot.

00:30:46.415 --> 00:30:48.790
And it can be hard sometimes,
because suddenly, something

00:30:48.790 --> 00:30:50.540
really interesting is
happening over there

00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:52.270
and you want to run
over and get it,

00:30:52.270 --> 00:30:55.000
and you need to be responsive
to that and obviously pay

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:56.090
attention to it.

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:59.175
But if you get too
caught up in the moment,

00:30:59.175 --> 00:31:00.550
you end up with
nothing, and this

00:31:00.550 --> 00:31:02.258
is something that is
a real struggle even

00:31:02.258 --> 00:31:04.420
for professional camera
people sometimes,

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:07.540
because they have all of this
information bombarding them

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:10.947
about what they want
to do and some of them,

00:31:10.947 --> 00:31:13.030
especially people who come
from still photography,

00:31:13.030 --> 00:31:15.490
maybe don't hold the
shots long enough.

00:31:15.490 --> 00:31:19.360
There's kind of a rule of the
nice long, slow count to 10

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:21.820
before you then pan
away to something else.

00:31:21.820 --> 00:31:26.290
Could be count to five if the
action is a little faster.

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:30.110
But just remember, frame
something up, hold the shot,

00:31:30.110 --> 00:31:32.420
and then move on.

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:34.840
And then the idea also when
you're moving the camera

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:38.200
is think about why
you're moving the camera.

00:31:38.200 --> 00:31:40.660
Why would you move
the camera rather than

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:43.460
just to hose down the room?

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:44.540
OK, any basic ideas?

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:49.350
Well, if I'm moving--

00:31:49.350 --> 00:31:53.757
if I have a camera, why would
I actually start doing this?

00:31:53.757 --> 00:31:54.840
Why would I actually move?

00:31:58.570 --> 00:32:04.480
Well, compared to just moving
like this randomly or not

00:32:04.480 --> 00:32:05.810
moving at all.

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:09.980
What would motivate me to
move the camera around?

00:32:09.980 --> 00:32:10.480
Right.

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:11.430
Yeah, exactly.

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:13.860
So following an action.

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:15.720
And it might be actually
a physical action.

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:19.260
I mean, if I'm profiling
Chris and suddenly she

00:32:19.260 --> 00:32:24.180
gets up and goes over to
really getting Dave's face

00:32:24.180 --> 00:32:27.120
to really argue
with them, I would

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:28.800
want to follow them, right?

00:32:28.800 --> 00:32:31.650
But if they're just
talking even cordially,

00:32:31.650 --> 00:32:34.930
I don't know why I always
bring you to conflict, David.

00:32:34.930 --> 00:32:35.730
I don't--

00:32:35.730 --> 00:32:37.000
TA: It's just who I am.

00:32:37.000 --> 00:32:41.250
PROFESSOR: Yeah, you just
draw it out of people.

00:32:41.250 --> 00:32:45.150
But even if they're just
they're speaking, as you say,

00:32:45.150 --> 00:32:47.260
I would be panning
back and forth.

00:32:47.260 --> 00:32:53.630
And at some point, I might
come over here and film Dave

00:32:53.630 --> 00:32:55.400
and start filming from
his point of view.

00:32:58.840 --> 00:33:01.945
So that's the situation.

00:33:01.945 --> 00:33:04.210
You want to try to make
your moves motivated

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:08.290
by something not just by
random hosing down of the room.

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:13.000
So that's, again, just a really
basic concept, and all of this

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:15.505
is-- it's going to make a
lot more sense when you start

00:33:15.505 --> 00:33:17.380
shooting, and it's really
going to make sense

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:20.750
when you start editing, because
editing is the part where

00:33:20.750 --> 00:33:22.000
you really learn how to shoot.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:25.120
I think all people
who shoot should

00:33:25.120 --> 00:33:27.160
edit, even if they don't
want to be editors,

00:33:27.160 --> 00:33:29.513
because that's when you
really learn what's working

00:33:29.513 --> 00:33:31.180
and what's not working
in your shooting.

00:33:33.940 --> 00:33:37.480
A couple of other quick tips.

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:42.430
Remember that although film
is a two dimensional art form

00:33:42.430 --> 00:33:46.910
and your images are all 2D,
you're representing a 3D world,

00:33:46.910 --> 00:33:49.210
and that means that
you want to think

00:33:49.210 --> 00:33:52.870
about ways to create depth
in your frame, which means,

00:33:52.870 --> 00:33:56.780
essentially, different
planes of action.

00:33:56.780 --> 00:34:01.030
So again, if I'm
filming Chris and Dave,

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:03.070
if I come around
like this, and I

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:05.440
shoot Chris's shoulder in
the foreground and Dave

00:34:05.440 --> 00:34:08.920
in the far background, that's
kind of a nice deep shot,

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:11.552
because we get a
sense of the room,

00:34:11.552 --> 00:34:12.969
we get a sense
that there's depth,

00:34:12.969 --> 00:34:17.300
that there's a dimensionality
to the conversation.

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:19.810
And then I can go over and
I can do the same this way.

00:34:19.810 --> 00:34:24.610
I also might still shoot close
ups and edit it all together.

00:34:24.610 --> 00:34:27.998
But what would be
weird and disorienting

00:34:27.998 --> 00:34:30.040
would be, again, if they're
having a conversation

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:33.790
and I just filmed a close up
of Chris and then I went over

00:34:33.790 --> 00:34:36.100
and I filmed a close
up of Dave and I never

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:38.949
had any sense of the
relationship in space

00:34:38.949 --> 00:34:42.730
or what the room was like or
anything else, because then

00:34:42.730 --> 00:34:46.239
you just have this head
floating in space over here,

00:34:46.239 --> 00:34:48.760
head floating in
space over there

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:50.929
with no kind of
relationship between them

00:34:50.929 --> 00:34:53.080
and no kind of three
dimensionality.

00:34:53.080 --> 00:34:56.920
So again, using depth
of field, using--

00:34:56.920 --> 00:34:59.230
even if something is out
of focus in the background

00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:00.130
or in the foreground.

00:35:00.130 --> 00:35:04.472
You could also have the
foreground be out of focus.

00:35:04.472 --> 00:35:06.430
So if there was something
on this computer that

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:08.410
was really, really
interesting and I

00:35:08.410 --> 00:35:11.350
wanted to have Dave as well,
I could have this be out

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:12.517
of focus and Dave in-focus.

00:35:12.517 --> 00:35:15.100
It doesn't have to be that the
foreground is the one the thing

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:15.767
that's in focus.

00:35:18.290 --> 00:35:19.950
And then the last
tip, and then I'm

00:35:19.950 --> 00:35:21.200
going to turn it over to Dave.

00:35:21.200 --> 00:35:23.480
And again, I understand
that this is all

00:35:23.480 --> 00:35:26.930
abstract at this
point, but it's just--

00:35:26.930 --> 00:35:29.930
I want to put some ideas out
there that will hopefully

00:35:29.930 --> 00:35:33.920
inform some of the choices
that you'll be making later.

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:35.420
But the last thing
I want to mention

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:41.060
is framing in terms of centering
things or not centering things.

00:35:41.060 --> 00:35:45.180
We tend to put
things off center.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:49.380
This is an interview that we did
with Joel Dawson, a professor

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:51.210
in electrical engineering here.

00:35:51.210 --> 00:35:57.840
Kind of dark on the screen, but
you see that he's off center,

00:35:57.840 --> 00:36:01.230
and there's a lot of negative
space on the left, which

00:36:01.230 --> 00:36:02.790
is where he's looking.

00:36:02.790 --> 00:36:04.620
And that tends to
be-- and I'm talking

00:36:04.620 --> 00:36:07.865
about very conventional
kinds of filming.

00:36:07.865 --> 00:36:09.240
That tends to be
something that's

00:36:09.240 --> 00:36:13.590
a little more pleasing, because
it gives the frame some more

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:15.870
energy.

00:36:15.870 --> 00:36:18.300
Psychologically, I'm not
exactly sure why that's true,

00:36:18.300 --> 00:36:21.120
but it might be that there's
space for him to potentially

00:36:21.120 --> 00:36:23.850
move into rather than
being lined up right

00:36:23.850 --> 00:36:25.860
in the center of the frame.

00:36:25.860 --> 00:36:31.530
So he's looking into space that
he could potentially occupy.

00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:33.990
Also, just as an aside,
notice that the background

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:36.290
is very, very soft.

00:36:36.290 --> 00:36:40.290
It's shot with quite a
long lens, which is nice

00:36:40.290 --> 00:36:44.400
because the interview subject
pops out number 1, and also

00:36:44.400 --> 00:36:48.450
number 2, it was this very,
very unattractive lab.

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:50.390
And it just goes away.

00:36:50.390 --> 00:36:52.510
It's very nice.

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:56.370
The other thing about
filming things off like this

00:36:56.370 --> 00:37:00.820
is that you want to think
about, in terms of framing,

00:37:00.820 --> 00:37:03.900
and this is, again,
classic composition.

00:37:03.900 --> 00:37:05.340
Think about your
frame in thirds.

00:37:08.190 --> 00:37:11.790
The third dividing lines--

00:37:11.790 --> 00:37:14.580
if you take a line and
divide it vertically

00:37:14.580 --> 00:37:17.010
and thirds and then
there's one line missing

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:20.190
here but horizontally in thirds,
the intersections of the lines

00:37:20.190 --> 00:37:23.010
tend to be powerful
places in the frame.

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:26.730
And you notice here, again,
it's very classic framing.

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:30.570
His eye lines up exactly
with one of those third

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:33.570
places, one of
those intersections.

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:37.170
That's why when you have
close ups of people in movies

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:38.670
and it gets really,
really close,

00:37:38.670 --> 00:37:40.140
they tend to chop
top of the head

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:43.350
off because they're keeping
the eye line roughly

00:37:43.350 --> 00:37:45.300
in the top third of the frame.

00:37:45.300 --> 00:37:47.350
You notice, again,
he's centered this way,

00:37:47.350 --> 00:37:49.715
so there's a lot of
negative space over there.

00:37:49.715 --> 00:37:51.090
You could just as
easily put them

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:54.360
over here looking this way
into the negative space

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:57.280
and his eye could be
lined up with that.

00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:00.300
If it was a wider shot,
like a very wide shot, maybe

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:04.200
a head to body shot and
we saw the entire frame,

00:38:04.200 --> 00:38:09.000
you would probably-- again,
if it was classic framing,

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:12.090
his head would end up being
in one of these third spaces

00:38:12.090 --> 00:38:13.350
as well.

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:17.820
So imagine a whole body
with a head here or here.

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:21.570
And if they were walking and
you were panning with them,

00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:24.810
if he was walking this way, you
would probably frame them up

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:27.120
on that line with his head
there, which would give him

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:29.160
plenty of space to walk into.

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:31.710
And then you would continue
to pan with him, always

00:38:31.710 --> 00:38:34.080
giving more space here,
because again, that gives you

00:38:34.080 --> 00:38:37.350
this energetic frame in the
sense that the person is

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:40.830
walking and you're following.

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:45.200
So that's the very, very
quick tour of some things

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:47.660
to think about when you
frame and choose shots,

00:38:47.660 --> 00:38:51.390
and we will be spending a
lot of time in this class.

00:38:51.390 --> 00:38:55.370
So don't think that you needed
to absorb it all right now.

00:38:55.370 --> 00:38:57.500
But-- go ahead.

00:38:57.500 --> 00:38:59.280
AUDIENCE: One of
things we realized

00:38:59.280 --> 00:39:01.190
about having them
to be videotaped is

00:39:01.190 --> 00:39:03.023
that in the future, it
becomes a chance then

00:39:03.023 --> 00:39:04.565
that after you've
been playing around

00:39:04.565 --> 00:39:06.760
with the camera at some
point, you can go back

00:39:06.760 --> 00:39:09.266
and to have relistened
to this talk when--

00:39:09.266 --> 00:39:10.516
it made more sense afterwards.

00:39:10.516 --> 00:39:12.310
PROFESSOR: Right, right.

00:39:12.310 --> 00:39:14.350
This will make the
most sense when,

00:39:14.350 --> 00:39:16.360
as I said, not only
done some shooting

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:18.250
but also done some editing.

00:39:18.250 --> 00:39:21.910
And between now and
then, we will definitely

00:39:21.910 --> 00:39:24.250
be going back to talk more
about film language and film

00:39:24.250 --> 00:39:26.860
grammar and the kinds of
shots that cut together

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:29.920
and the kinds of shots
that don't cut together.

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:32.180
I'm going to turn it over
to Dave in just a minute,

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:36.100
but I want to say one
last word about formats.

00:39:36.100 --> 00:39:39.820
So formats in video are
proliferating like crazy.

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:45.190
There are an endless number
of HD SD, but at this point

00:39:45.190 --> 00:39:47.980
primarily HD high
definition formats.

00:39:47.980 --> 00:39:50.530
The one we're filming
or using in this class

00:39:50.530 --> 00:39:55.270
is HDV 1080i 1440 by
1080, which is basically

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:59.740
a compressed version of full
HD, which is 1920 by 1080.

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:02.680
And you may know something
about what those things mean,

00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:04.480
you may know nothing
about what they mean,

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:07.780
but I want you to
write them down

00:40:07.780 --> 00:40:11.170
and you'll absorb them
later if that makes sense.

00:40:11.170 --> 00:40:16.300
Basically, 1920 by 1080 refers
to the size of the frame.

00:40:16.300 --> 00:40:22.990
It's 1,920 pixels wide by
1080 pixels high, which again,

00:40:22.990 --> 00:40:26.650
if we go back to this
frame, that gives you

00:40:26.650 --> 00:40:29.320
basically that aspect ratio.

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:33.420
So it's wider than it is high.

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:36.060
And that's 16 by 9
televisions, as opposed

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:40.620
to standard definition
square or televisions.

00:40:40.620 --> 00:40:44.880
Our data rate is 25%
megabits per second,

00:40:44.880 --> 00:40:48.810
and that boils down to
approximately more or less

00:40:48.810 --> 00:40:51.730
10 gigabytes per hour.

00:40:51.730 --> 00:40:56.010
And what that means, obviously,
is that the file sizes

00:40:56.010 --> 00:40:57.930
are very, very large.

00:40:57.930 --> 00:40:59.730
There's a lot of data.

00:40:59.730 --> 00:41:02.820
The camera's capturing a lot of
data, the computer you edit on

00:41:02.820 --> 00:41:05.065
is expected to
process a lot of data.

00:41:05.065 --> 00:41:07.440
And one of the things that we
will be talking about later

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:11.580
in the class is how the
IT revolution has really

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:15.420
enabled video documentaries
and completely changed the game

00:41:15.420 --> 00:41:18.670
and enabled things like
this class to happen.

00:41:18.670 --> 00:41:20.670
The point here is mainly
just so that you're

00:41:20.670 --> 00:41:23.850
kind of aware of the format
that these cameras shoot

00:41:23.850 --> 00:41:27.252
and what the file
sizes are going to be.

00:41:27.252 --> 00:41:28.710
That's also why we
give each of you

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:30.168
a hard drive so
that you don't have

00:41:30.168 --> 00:41:32.520
to worry about how
you're going to store

00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:35.380
a terabyte worth of
material on your own.

00:41:35.380 --> 00:41:38.040
So any quick questions before
I turn it over to Dave?

00:41:44.850 --> 00:41:45.582
Yeah?

00:41:45.582 --> 00:41:50.010
AUDIENCE: Is there--
the first one there--

00:41:50.010 --> 00:41:52.610
so the framing-- how
to get their shape.

00:41:52.610 --> 00:41:54.810
PROFESSOR: Yeah, it's
basically squeezed.

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:56.580
But you never see it squeezed.

00:41:56.580 --> 00:41:58.980
It actually then gets
uncompressed automatically

00:41:58.980 --> 00:41:59.930
when it's viewed.

00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:01.380
AUDIENCE: So we see as 1920?

00:42:01.380 --> 00:42:04.440
PROFESSOR: Yes, you see the
same it looks like 1920 by 1080,

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:06.720
and the reason that they
compress it like that

00:42:06.720 --> 00:42:09.610
is to save space.

00:42:09.610 --> 00:42:12.210
So the 25 megabits
per second data rate

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:17.610
is very, very low for
high definition video.

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:22.980
Most data rates are higher
than that, like 35, 50, 55,

00:42:22.980 --> 00:42:25.530
or for completely
uncompressed HD,

00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:29.100
really I forget it's
really, really high.

00:42:29.100 --> 00:42:33.180
So it's just a strategy
for giving less data

00:42:33.180 --> 00:42:36.900
but still having
a nice HD image.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:38.900
You never actually
see that, unless you

00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:42.590
make a mistake in final cut,
which we can talk about later.

00:42:42.590 --> 00:42:45.050
And just one word
about why we're using

00:42:45.050 --> 00:42:48.950
tape in this era
of cards and files.

00:42:48.950 --> 00:42:51.350
One reason is that the cameras
are a couple of years old,

00:42:51.350 --> 00:42:52.808
but there's actually
another reason

00:42:52.808 --> 00:42:55.910
to which is that this provides
a very cheap and easy backup

00:42:55.910 --> 00:42:57.800
for you.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:43:01.970
File management, as wonderful
as cards are and as much

00:43:01.970 --> 00:43:06.080
as it speeds the process and I'm
a big fan of them, in a student

00:43:06.080 --> 00:43:11.320
situation, if you want to do
cheap backups, tape is great.

00:43:11.320 --> 00:43:13.070
TA: You can stick them
in your sock drawer

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:15.290
and not have to worry
about your hard drive

00:43:15.290 --> 00:43:17.680
crashing or something.

